gton Post  meaning basically we gave them a piece  that we've been working on for a while  about lawyers who blow the deadline for  death penalty cases meaning that their  clients  the last and most important round of  Appeals and I like that story for a  couple of reasons one of them was it's  big and complicated and I think you know  the it's always a challenge to do  complicated well and that and something  that I've always regarded as a one of  the highest ambitions of journalism and  also it was a story that was mostly  about guilty people it's very easy to  generate sympathy for the wrong  wrongfully convicted  you know the Innocence Project does  heroic work but the criminal justice  system mostly engages people who are  guilty and nonetheless it it fails us  you know what led you to leave your  comfortable perch at the Times I mean  you had to earn your column and  everything to start for a you know a  kind of moderately funded or you know  tentative tenuously funded smaller  functioning news startup you mean you  want to hear about my midlife crisis if  that's what it was you know it wasn't a  midlife crisis although it was a I guess  partly motivated by a desire to have an  next act I mean I was at the Times for  30 years  I had some of the best jobs in the world  first and foremost being a foreign  correspondent in times of real people  it looks South Africa in South Africa in  Russia you know I mean you can't  complain about a perch as a hotbed  columnist at the New York Times you've  got a great audience and all kinds of  freedom but I did find that I sort of  missed just the sitting around with a  bunch of smart people trying to figure  out a problem the problem might be how  are you gonna cover the Arab Spring or  it might be although I'm hearing you  saying there aren't any smart people  left at the New York Times now there are  lots of lower lots of smart people at  the New York Times but we better call on  this role there is pretty much lonely  yeah you don't spend a lot of time  consulting you know in your fascinating  2013 online debate with Glenn Greenwald  about journalism and what makes for good  or bad journalism you expressed a the  view that suspending passion helps make  for more effective better journalism and  there's a variety of interesting pieces  on the site relate it's a criminal  justice reform but the framing and the  headlines are pretty dispassionate we  cover reason covers the same issues from  the same perspective but with headlines  meant to in  kind of outraged internet you know and  provoke provoke empathy and you know be  provoked  I mean basically what what's going on  with your ethos they're like how you  know what's the play that you're making  I mean first of all I should say I know  that you guys have a passionate interest  in criminal justice because Andrew Cohen  who does our daily runs later dotes on  go great on the links to reason my  instincts are to try to keep the  temperature low to have a rational  discussion not a screaming match but we  you know I do like headlines that make  you want to read the story I mean  everybody seems to be consuming more  news from more sources or at least more  information or more media do you think  that people read errs and consumers of  media are more sophisticated now and  there or are they you know it is the  kind of a BuzzFeed or and up where these  style headline you know what clickbait  headline the only way to really catch  people's attention you know I like to  think that people are sophisticated  enough to actually pick and choose  serious in-depth reporting and and I  mean the evidence is an awful lot of  them are drawn to cat videos and you  know buzzy headlines and listicles you  know I'm not above using a listicle if  it will get somebody to read about the  death penalty or the fraud condition of  police community relations in the debate  with Greenwald use he said that  objectivity is a suffocating constraint  on how reporters are permitted to  express themselves and it produces a  self neutering form of journalism that  and he said that becomes as ineffectual  as it is boring you know give your  capsule of response to that is it is he  right or is he is he partly right as he  just off you know off the reservation  well no I mean he's he's partly right I  mean look I read a lot of polemical  writing I enjoy it I like a good  argument you know it gets the blood  flowing and it makes people think that's  great I read Glenn Greenwald but I think  that there's a really important place  for journalism that sees as its  obligation to get the readers trust  right and you do that  by sort of suppressing you know I don't  like to use the word objective because  it conjures up some sort of  philosophical perfect state of truth I  prefer impartial but I think to make a  deliberate discipline out of laying  aside your Compassion's convictions and  prejudices and to try to see your way to  through to the truth  small team I think is an extremely  valuable discipline it doesn't mean that  you fall into false equivalencies I mean  you know the the evidence will often  tell you that one point of view has more  merit than and and your work I mean at  the Marshall project it's not as if this  form of objectivity or this form of kind  of measured analysis doesn't actually  have a point of view because I mean the  stories you tell all kind of come to the  same conclusion right that the criminal  justice system is in desperate need of  reform right there most of the stories  are about dysfunction in the system  although we're making a concerted effort  to find examples of things that work  which we'll look at skeptically but  we'll highlight them when we find them  do you think that in terms of social  change that gets a larger project of  social change I mean you have political  activism and advocacy of policy change  and then within the journalism spaces  that often a function of kind of  overlapping like people moving in the  same direction so you might have  activist bloggers as doing one thing in  polemical journalist and then people who  are doing more along the lines of what  you're doing and that that is is it kind  of the gestalt of that that actually  changes things yeah I think that's right  I think journalism you know if the  actual change is at least the final  stages of it is in the hands of people  who are elected who people who make  policy and advocates who have a draft  legislation for them but what  journalists can do is create a context  where the advocates get listened to and  the policymakers get persuaded and  that's true both of the you know  straight-ahead journalism that we try to  practice and the more polemical  journalism you're working now at a 501c3  a non  research and educational institution do  you think that's the future of  journalism or a big future of journal a  big part of the future for journalism as  a kind of funding mechanism is  nonprofits yo I think it's a future I  don't know how big I don't think I mean  anybody who tells you they know what the  future of journalism is now is is blowin  smoke I like to refer to us as nonprofit  on purpose  distinct from the rest of the media  which is just nonprofit although I mean  even at the times it's not so is it that  it's not profitable or it's that it's  not as now I'm being yeah I'm that way  and obviously but talk a little bit  about that because that seems I mean to  go from say a 30% return on investment  to a 10 percent return on investment it  seems to be cataclysmic whereas most  industries in America or you know lucky  to make any money so how does that how  does that like create such a earthquake  in journalism well first of all people  had sort of built their lives around the  30% return on investment and second of  all an awful lot of journalistic  ventures these days are not anywhere  close to 10% return on investment or any  return on investment and you know and  for most people in this business it's  personal you've seen friends laid off  you've seen newspapers scrap their print  editions or go to twice a week you've  seen the kind of stories that really  should be covered you know state  capitals state legislatures you know  going uncovered which are you know  places that somebody really ought to be  paying close attention to all right well  we'll leave it there for Reason TV I'm  Nick Gillespie and we've been talking to  Bill Keller who's heading up the  Marshall project which is a great  nonprofit investigative journalism  outfit dedicated to criminal justice  reform thanks Bob you're welcome

